{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790980720,"nonce":"pd0OxLIO+ndN+hV+h76cj5+Bu2E=","message":"A drop now reads as it was drawn, which closes the consequence D8 recorded.\n\nThe rule: on a drop that writes a rank — list, board or gantt — every row\ndrawn **above the drop point in the same ordering scope** that has no rank\ngets one, in its drawn order, before the dragged row's own key is computed\nbetween its neighbours. The scope is the one `lessByRank` already orders\nwithin: on a list and a gantt the same group and, nested, the same parent\n(`siblingAt`); on a board the same stack, which is one lane's one column.\nRows *below* the drop point are left unranked — they already sort after\neverything ranked, in the query's own order, which is exactly where they are\ndrawn, so ranking them would be writes that change nothing.\n\nEach filled row is its own `host.IssueSet`, one commit per issue, because\nthere is no multi-entity commit; the dragged row's own set still carries its\nrank and, where it crossed, the `columns`/`group_by` field, in one commit.\nThe status line counts them: `rank set · 2 ranked`. A view where everything\nis already ranked fills nothing and is the single write it always was.\n\nThe alternative, ranking a whole scope on its first drop, was rejected: a\ndrag of one issue should not rewrite a backlog of three hundred, and the\nrows below the drop point need no key to stay put.\n\nCode is `tui/rank.go` (`fillRanks`, `scopeFills`, `writeFills`), wired into\n`tui/list.go`, `tui/board.go` and `tui/gantt.go`; tests in `tui/rank_test.go`.\nRecorded in `doc/design/configurable-schema.md` D8 and the Rank section of\n`doc/design/terminal-renderer.md`.","files":null}]}